==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|dʰeh₁}}
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|infect}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|infectus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|inficiō||dye, taint}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfɛkt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkt}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring into contact with a substance that causes illness (a [[pathogen]]).
#: {{ux|en|Not everyone will be '''infected''' when an epidemic strikes.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/katie-l-burke Katie L. Burke]
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/3/in-the-news-45 In the News]
|volume=101|issue=3|page=193|magazine={{w|American Scientist}}
|passage=Bats host many high-profile viruses that can '''infect''' humans, including severe acute respiratory syndrome and Ebola.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make somebody enthusiastic about one's own passion.
#: {{ux|en|Her passion for dancing has '''infected''' me.}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|disinfect}}

